Z||000002||DecadenceR||06-02-2001||12:49 AM||||Thanks for sending me that paint drawing...<BR>Now, I have my headliner off now and it looks like the water would come into the passanger compartment. Is it just around the sunroof that's sealed off?||||reg||1
Z||000003||cadaver||06-02-2001||02:29 AM||||The holes get drilled right in the corners near the gasket.<BR>No water wont come in the roof now. The area under the fiberglass roof is a wet area anyway.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Galen||06-02-2001||02:35 AM||||a picture would still be nice  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> if someone could post it here.. I am in need of dis too<P>------------------<BR>Galen - 1988 Hawaiian Orchid Coupe Auto 4Banger - <A HREF="http://myfiero.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero Website</A><BR>AIM: WookieeNC||||reg||1
Z||000005||Brucepts||06-02-2001||12:50 PM||||I drilled the holes in my 84 SE on Monday. It downpoured yesturday and last nite, no wet headliner for me anymore!!! Now I can finally replace the old headliner with a new one. <P>I drilled the holes on the inside corners of the seals, you have to pull them back a tad to get the drill in. Mine had some kinda sealant gooped in there that I had to dig out with a scribe (previous owner repair?). The gutter channel is rusted at all four corners also from the water laying in there. I vacuumed out all the rust so none of it would move into the new holes I drilled. Without this forum I'd still be getting a wet butt from my seat . . .  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Advanced Dealer in the Great Material Continuum . . .<BR>Non Fiero Website: <A HREF="http://tractorsport.com" TARGET=_blank>http://tractorsport.com</A> <BR>You can't race a dyno . . .||||reg||1
Z||000006||Galen||06-02-2001||01:34 PM||||How can you get rust in a fiberglass top? Do you mean you drilled the holes in the SEAL or in  the channel?<P>------------------<BR>Galen - 1988 Hawaiian Orchid Coupe Auto 4Banger - <A HREF="http://myfiero.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero Website</A><BR>AIM: WookieeNC||||reg||1
Z||000007||Brucepts||06-02-2001||01:44 PM||||The channel that holds the seal on my 84 is steel. The holes are drilled right in front (meaning on the interior side of the car) of the seal corner not in the seal itself. Once you see the seal and how it is installed you will see where you need to drill. Its really not that hard to figureout once you see it. Any water that gets by the seal runs down the channel and drains out the new holes and not into the interior.<P>------------------<BR>Advanced Dealer in the Great Material Continuum . . .<BR>Non Fiero Website: <A HREF="http://tractorsport.com" TARGET=_blank>http://tractorsport.com</A> <BR>You can't race a dyno . . .||||reg||1
Z||000008||Galen||06-02-2001||01:53 PM||||Just went and did it, I see what you are talking about. It rained last night, I drilled em and it drained..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> cool.. <P>what size drillbit did you use?<P>------------------<BR>Galen - 1988 Hawaiian Orchid Coupe Auto 4Banger - <A HREF="http://myfiero.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero Website</A><BR>AIM: WookieeNC||||reg||1
